00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:09.000
It's because M is a keyboard shortcut used for something else in Blender, and so

223
00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:12.871
they use G for grab. So you're grabbing and moving the object.

224
00:16:12.901 --> 00:16:17.850
That's what G stands for, okay? And so once you grab it, you can move it wherever

225
00:16:17.911 --> 00:16:19.440
you want in 3D space.

226
00:16:21.090 --> 00:16:24.570
If you're in the middle of a command and you want to cancel it, you hit the escape

227
00:16:24.661 --> 00:16:29.760
key, and that brings it back to where it was before you moved it around.

228
00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:36.600
And if you move it, G, and I left mouse click and leave it at a

229
00:16:36.661 --> 00:16:41.010
new position, and you want to go back, Control Z or Command Z will bring you back

230
00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:45.600
to, will undo the command. Okay? This is the same as many other software out there.

231
00:16:46.651 --> 00:16:50.490
All right? Now, what's cool about it is, and the reason why I spent so much time on

232
00:16:50.760 --> 00:16:56.401
X, Y, and Z earlier is you can constrain the motion of the cube or the

233
00:16:56.520 --> 00:17:02.280
