00:32:33.052 --> 00:32:37.671
like if you render it, okay? Here's where we go back to the properties menu, the

439
00:32:37.761 --> 00:32:42.711
very top icon, the second to top icon, which looks like the back of the DSLR.

440
00:32:45.111 --> 00:32:49.942
Okay, right now we are in EV mode. That's probably why the, what do you call it?

441
00:32:50.002 --> 00:32:52.701
The transparency is not showing up very well.

442
00:32:53.361 --> 00:32:59.181
By the way, EV was what was used in the movie Flow to do the entirety of its

443
00:32:59.451 --> 00:33:05.151
animation rendering. I have a reasonably good computer with a graphics card, so I'm

444
00:33:05.181 --> 00:33:09.861
going to go and use Cycles, which is a more advanced, more realistic rendering

445
00:33:09.921 --> 00:33:15.502
mode. And in fact, I'm going to make sure that my GPU is involved in the rendering

446
00:33:15.562 --> 00:33:20.961
process. And I'm going to just do a sample render here for you.

447
00:33:21.082 --> 00:33:25.401
So max samples, I'm just going to reduce this to 32 for now to make this go a

448
00:33:25.431 --> 00:33:28.582
little bit faster. And to render out this image,

449
00:33:30.111 --> 00:33:33.291
you need to set the camera to look at this image.

450
00:33:33.711 --> 00:33:35.752
This is going to go fast, my apologies.

451
00:33:36.141 --> 00:33:36.771
So View,

452
00:33:38.211 --> 00:33:42.981
Align View, Align Active Camera to View, like so.

453
00:33:43.821 --> 00:33:48.291
And then I need to hit the N key to pop out the side here, go to View,

454
00:33:49.552 --> 00:33:53.391
and hit Lock to 3D Cursor and Camera to View.

455
00:33:53.420 --> 00:33:59.391
And then I can zoom in and out to position my object nicely into my view port as I

456
00:33:59.451 --> 00:34:03.832
want it. Okay, we'll leave it like that. N key to hide the side panel.

457
00:34:04.312 --> 00:34:07.101
And then you go to Render, Render Image,

458
00:34:08.391 --> 00:34:09.681
and it will render out your image.

459
00:34:13.401 --> 00:34:18.741
Okay, and you can see now that it's glass and plastic, okay, as we tried to make

460
00:34:18.802 --> 00:34:22.641
it. Of course, you could spend a lot more time on lighting and material adjustments

461
00:34:22.672 --> 00:34:25.521
to get this to be more beautiful, but this is the beginning of it.

462
00:34:25.881 --> 00:34:31.042
Once you have a rendering that you like, if you want to actually download it for

463
00:34:31.192 --> 00:34:36.411
use in your documentation or whatever, you can go to Image, Save, and then

464
00:34:36.621 --> 00:34:40.461
obviously choose a place. I'm going to save it into downloads, and I'll call it

465
00:34:40.521 --> 00:34:42.471
tutorial render.

466
00:34:45.172 --> 00:34:49.911
Okay, so now if I go to my Windows Explorer,

467
00:34:51.651 --> 00:34:56.361
go to Downloads, I have a file here called tutorial render that I can-- Are you

468
00:34:56.422 --> 00:35:00.052
looking at it? I'm hoping you see it. You seeing my Windows Explorer? Yes, you are.

469
00:35:00.442 --> 00:35:03.861
Okay, so I double-click on tutorial image.

470
00:35:03.922 --> 00:35:05.001
You'll see that

471
00:35:08.601 --> 00:35:10.221
I have this image that I rendered out.

472
00:35:13.071 --> 00:35:18.531
All right, so that was a ridiculously fast tour through the fundamentals of

473
00:35:18.591 --> 00:35:19.071
Blender.

474
00:35:20.811 --> 00:35:25.911
How many of you are panicking that you regret your decision to join this session

475
00:35:25.942 --> 00:35:26.361
today?

476
00:35:29.661 --> 00:35:30.951
All right, so far so good?

477
00:35:33.141 --> 00:35:33.381
Okay.

478
00:35:36.501 --> 00:35:42.442
All right, I'm going to hide my lovely plane and perfume

479
00:35:42.501 --> 00:35:44.511
bottle, and I'm going to model up something else.

480
00:35:44.571 --> 00:35:49.521
I'm going to model up a very simple cartoon character and to show you some other

481
00:35:49.552 --> 00:35:51.922
techniques that you will need if you want to model up

482
00:35:53.841 --> 00:35:59.631
clothing or whatever. Okay. One of the key aspects of this week is mesh

483
00:35:59.661 --> 00:36:03.531
manipulation, and Blender is a mesh manipulation software.

484
00:36:03.861 --> 00:36:09.561
So let me give you an example. The plane that we used to be the background or the

485
00:36:09.621 --> 00:36:11.361
floor of our scene

486
00:36:12.771 --> 00:36:13.521
is just

487
00:36:14.781 --> 00:36:16.821
as it is indicated. It's just a flat

488
00:36:18.201 --> 00:36:19.672
square plane. If I hit

489
00:36:20.991 --> 00:36:23.961
Tab to go into edit mode, I see that it has four corners.

490
00:36:24.381 --> 00:36:27.711
So this is a mesh with zero subdivisions, okay?

491
00:36:28.461 --> 00:36:32.091
Now I'm going to turn it into a real mesh by right mouse clicking and hitting

492
00:36:32.151 --> 00:36:34.942
Subdivide. So now instead of being one

493
00:36:36.172 --> 00:36:39.501
plane with one shape inside, it's got four planes.

494
00:36:39.891 --> 00:36:44.811
I'm going to do that again, subdivide one more time, one more time, one more time.

495
00:36:45.459 --> 00:36:50.650
So this is a mesh. It is a surface with lots of control points on top of it.

496
00:36:50.769 --> 00:36:56.469
So if I go back, hit one to go into vertex mode, this surface now has lots and lots

497
00:36:56.499 --> 00:37:00.549
of points that I can manipulate. So imagine this is a handkerchief and I want to

498
00:37:00.609 --> 00:37:03.400
make it look all flowy and stuff like that.

499
00:37:03.819 --> 00:37:08.139
I can go in here and select a couple of these points, shift key and select a few of

500
00:37:08.230 --> 00:37:13.689
these vertices and do GZ to move it up. Okay?

501
00:37:14.289 --> 00:37:18.129
But that's not very satisfying because only those points are moving up.

502
00:37:18.699 --> 00:37:23.199
So what you can do is there's a button up in the top here, which is a circle shape,

503
00:37:23.739 --> 00:37:28.989
or the shortcut key O, will allow you to do something called proportional editing.

504
00:37:29.469 --> 00:37:33.519
Proportional editing will allow you to not only manipulate the

505
00:37:35.139 --> 00:37:39.159
vertices that you just selected, but its surrounding neighbors as well to the

506
00:37:39.219 --> 00:37:44.349
degree that you control. Okay? So now if I do GZ, you'll see that there's a gray

507
00:37:44.439 --> 00:37:49.569
circle there that if I roll my mouse button, it gets bigger and smaller.

508
00:37:49.989 --> 00:37:52.419
This gray circle is the area of influence.

509
00:37:52.900 --> 00:37:54.879
So the move before-- So I'm going to turn off

510
00:37:56.379 --> 00:38:01.119
proportional editing. And once again, if I do GZ to move these dots upward, it only

511
00:38:01.179 --> 00:38:05.499
pulls up those dots, and every other dot around it doesn't get affected at all.

512
00:38:05.859 --> 00:38:11.199
However, if I turn on proportional editing with O or click this button up here and

513
00:38:11.230 --> 00:38:15.939
do GZ, depending on the size of my area of influence, it will bring up its

514
00:38:15.999 --> 00:38:16.869
neighbors as well.

515
00:38:18.279 --> 00:38:22.329
Okay? So if I make the circle smaller, the influence is smaller.

516
00:38:22.418 --> 00:38:27.909
If I make the circle bigger, it influences more of the surface, the

517
00:38:27.999 --> 00:38:29.769
mesh that I'm playing with.

518
00:38:31.749 --> 00:38:35.109
Cool, right? I think that this should be a wow moment for some of you.

519
00:38:35.379 --> 00:38:40.989
Okay, so one vertex. Now I'm going to try to manipulate this GZ one more time and

520
00:38:41.049 --> 00:38:44.199
reduce the area of influence and drag this up.

521
00:38:45.459 --> 00:38:49.839
And you can do it dynamically. As you pull it up, you can change what gets

522
00:38:49.869 --> 00:38:50.529
manipulated.

523
00:38:53.679 --> 00:38:58.419
So very cool. So proportional editing, shortcut key O. Okay?

524
00:39:00.879 --> 00:39:05.049
Mesh modeling. Okay, so now let's go-- I'm going to return to Control + Z to return

525
00:39:05.109 --> 00:39:06.789
all of these things back to normal.

526
00:39:08.049 --> 00:39:09.369
Tab to go back into

527
00:39:11.650 --> 00:39:15.369
object mode. I'm going to hide my plane, and now we're going to model up.

528
00:39:15.879 --> 00:39:20.589
How many of you know the game Among Us?

529
00:39:20.679 --> 00:39:24.309
Do you know this game Among Us? Any of you with children are smiling because you

530
00:39:24.369 --> 00:39:25.959
know very well what I'm talking about.

531
00:39:26.409 --> 00:39:31.119
Okay, you know what I'm going to bring up for the rest of you who are obviously not

532
00:39:31.209 --> 00:39:34.869
very cool, I need to teach you what Among Us is. Let me show you.

533
00:39:35.650 --> 00:39:39.609
It is a bean-shaped character that looks something like this. Okay?

534
00:39:40.329 --> 00:39:43.389
I use this to teach my kids how to do 3D modeling on Blender.

535
00:39:43.689 --> 00:39:45.999
So we're going to do an Among Us character right now.

536
00:39:47.349 --> 00:39:52.599
All right. So surprisingly, I'm going to Shift + A, add a cube. You're like, "What?

537
00:39:52.869 --> 00:39:56.650
It's a bean shape. Why the heck are you starting off with a cube?" The reason I

538
00:39:56.679 --> 00:39:59.139
start off with a cube is because the few--

539
00:40:00.998 --> 00:40:04.449
Think of modeling on Blender like you're sculpting.

540
00:40:04.869 --> 00:40:08.169
You're going to start off with a block of material, and you're going to create a

541
00:40:08.259 --> 00:40:13.119
rough shape of it first and then slowly refine it into its smooth,

542
00:40:13.269 --> 00:40:17.049
curvaceous face that you'll end up wanting to do.

543
00:40:17.439 --> 00:40:21.819
So if you look at pictures of people sculpting sculptures, the beginning shape of

544
00:40:21.879 --> 00:40:26.379
it is very, very rough. And the reason we want to do that is the fewer number of

545
00:40:26.469 --> 00:40:32.319
faces that are present in a Blender modeling situation, the less demand

546
00:40:32.379 --> 00:40:37.089
it will be on your computer. If you immediately put 1,000 subdivisions onto this

547
00:40:37.179 --> 00:40:41.079
cube and then you try to move it around, it's going to make your computer very,

548
00:40:41.139 --> 00:40:43.419
very slow, and it's going to heat up and get hot.

549
00:40:43.689 --> 00:40:48.759
So we're going to create this by using the least amount of shapes that we can.

550
00:40:49.299 --> 00:40:51.459
However, I'm going to use a modifier here.

551
00:40:51.759 --> 00:40:56.799
Because it's a bean shape, I'm going to go add modifier, generate subdivision

552
00:40:56.859 --> 00:41:00.819
surface to turn it into something that generally looks like a bean. Okay?

553
00:41:01.689 --> 00:41:03.129
And this is going to be my starting point.

554
00:41:04.449 --> 00:41:08.559
I'm going to change the level viewport from level one to level two to give it a

555
00:41:08.619 --> 00:41:11.169
little bit more subdivisions, but not too many.

556
00:41:11.859 --> 00:41:15.849
And then I'm going to apply this modifier. I'm happy.

557
00:41:16.209 --> 00:41:20.349
I want it to be round, so instead of leaving it non-destructive and preview, I can

558
00:41:20.409 --> 00:41:22.539
click on this down arrow, click Apply.

559
00:41:22.989 --> 00:41:24.999
Let me show you what happens when you don't apply it.

560
00:41:25.419 --> 00:41:31.359
So from object mode, if I hit Tab to go into edit mode, it still thinks of this

561
00:41:31.480 --> 00:41:35.529
object as a cube. Right? And we can actually manipulate it in this way.

562
00:41:35.889 --> 00:41:40.719
For instance, if I scale it in the Z direction, I can stretch this out to be more

563
00:41:40.749 --> 00:41:43.749
like the Among Us character while maintaining a cube.

564
00:41:43.809 --> 00:41:48.579
And it's still, because it thinks it's a cube, it's less demanding on the computer.

565
00:41:48.969 --> 00:41:53.349
Right? The Among Us character is more flat, so I'm going to scale it in the Y

566
00:41:53.379 --> 00:41:57.429
direction, S-Y to scale it, to flatten it a little bit like this.

567
00:41:58.659 --> 00:42:04.629
Okay? And G-Z to move it up above the work plane because he's going to have legs,

568
00:42:05.409 --> 00:42:08.919
and object mode to go back. All right, so that's the beginning of my Among Us

569
00:42:08.949 --> 00:42:13.239
character. But from this point, I want to be able to access all of these little

570
00:42:13.299 --> 00:42:14.499
subdivisions within

571
00:42:15.849 --> 00:42:20.650
the geometry I just made. So I'm going to have to apply this modifier.

572
00:42:20.739 --> 00:42:25.569
So go with the down arrow, hit Apply, and now when I hit Tab, I go back in.

573
00:42:25.629 --> 00:42:29.319
It's got all of the subdivisions baked into it. Okay?

574
00:42:30.309 --> 00:42:35.829
So as you saw, I think, let me see. If I go back to Among Us one more time.

575
00:42:40.659 --> 00:42:44.319
This character is very, very simple. It's got only a few features.

576
00:42:44.379 --> 00:42:48.159
It's got a face, it's got a backpack, and it's got two legs.

577
00:42:48.579 --> 00:42:50.049
So that's what we're going to do to model.

578
00:42:50.589 --> 00:42:55.389
And because it's symmetrical, I don't want to have to model two arms, two legs, and

579
00:42:55.449 --> 00:42:59.619
so on and so forth. What I can do is to model only half of this object.

580
00:43:00.519 --> 00:43:02.019
So here I am in

581
00:43:03.939 --> 00:43:09.009
edit mode with minus Y. Okay, I want to make sure minus Y is facing in this

582
00:43:09.039 --> 00:43:13.058
direction, and I know here, minus Y. So this is the front of my guy.

583
00:43:16.299 --> 00:43:20.559
There's a button here, which is two squares overlapping one another.

584
00:43:20.919 --> 00:43:26.049
This is X-ray mode. So if I click on that, I can see the dots on my object, both in

585
00:43:26.079 --> 00:43:28.959
the front and the back, and I'll show you why this is important.

586
00:43:29.289 --> 00:43:33.789
If I don't have X-ray mode turned on and I look at this object directly from the

587
00:43:33.849 --> 00:43:39.669
front and I do a selection like this with a left mouse click drag,

588
00:43:40.209 --> 00:43:45.219
it selects all of the vertices that it can see, but all the vertices in the back

589
00:43:45.249 --> 00:43:49.119
that it cannot see is not selected. Okay? I don't want that.

590
00:43:49.179 --> 00:43:53.230
Right now, I want to be able to select front and back simultaneously, so I turn on

591
00:43:53.409 --> 00:43:56.199
X-ray mode, so I can see both front and back.

592
00:43:56.529 --> 00:44:01.719
And now when I do selection of half of the object, it selects everything from front

593
00:44:01.749 --> 00:44:04.089
to back. It's a very useful tool, okay?

594
00:44:04.629 --> 00:44:09.579
I'm going to delete half all of these things by hitting X for delete and choosing

595
00:44:09.639 --> 00:44:13.959
to delete faces, and I'm left with a half of a bean. Okay?

596
00:44:15.219 --> 00:44:16.959
And now I'm going to use another modifier.

597
00:44:16.959 --> 00:44:22.929
I'm going to select A to select all, go to add modifier, generate mirror, and

598
00:44:22.959 --> 00:44:24.699
it's going to create the other half for me.

599
00:44:25.179 --> 00:44:29.019
And make sure that when you use the mirror modifier, that you also select clipping.

600
00:44:29.409 --> 00:44:32.949
Okay? This is very important. A lot of you will forget to do this, and it creates a

601
00:44:33.009 --> 00:44:36.639
mess. Make sure you select clipping when you use the mirror modifier.

602
00:44:37.119 --> 00:44:42.999
Okay, so why am I doing this? Is because now when I make changes on this side, it

603
00:44:43.059 --> 00:44:46.599
automatically gets reflected on the other side as well. It saves me a lot of time.

604
00:44:48.939 --> 00:44:49.779
Okay, wonderful.

605
00:44:51.009 --> 00:44:54.369
Let's create a face. So how do I do that?

606
00:44:54.429 --> 00:44:57.400
Hit three to-- First of all, I'm going to turn off

607
00:44:58.900 --> 00:45:02.499
X-ray mode just so I don't accidentally select something in the back of the object.

608
00:45:03.189 --> 00:45:05.499
Hit three to do face select. I'm going to

609
00:45:07.029 --> 00:45:09.400
click and select these four faces.

610
00:45:09.639 --> 00:45:12.819
I can do that by holding down the shift key and just doing this.

611
00:45:13.209 --> 00:45:19.179
Or a faster way to do it is to left mouse click and drag and kind of intersect

612
00:45:19.239 --> 00:45:23.650
those four desirable things. And this is going to be your face, okay?

613
00:45:24.369 --> 00:45:28.659
With these four faces selected, you're going to hit E on your keyboard for extrude,

614
00:45:28.719 --> 00:45:31.299
and you're going to punch it out a little bit for the face.

615
00:45:32.109 --> 00:45:35.919
It's going to look like that. Now I'm going to go back to the back of the object,

616
00:45:36.009 --> 00:45:37.839
and I'm going to do a backpack. Okay?

617
00:45:37.959 --> 00:45:41.049
So once again, selecting the ones I want,

618
00:45:42.339 --> 00:45:45.279
I think. Yeah, that's good. And then do an E.

619
00:45:45.909 --> 00:45:51.129
And actually, you can control it by doing E-Y to make sure it extrudes directly

620
00:45:51.189 --> 00:45:51.730
backwards.

621
00:45:52.959 --> 00:45:54.159
Okay, so that's my backpack.

622
00:45:57.375 --> 00:46:02.715
And one thing, so far I've not done any saving whatsoever, and that's super, super

623
00:46:02.805 --> 00:46:07.755
dangerous because you will have done many, many, many things, and then you will try

624
00:46:07.815 --> 00:46:10.635
to do a render, and your computer will crash, and you'll lose everything.

625
00:46:10.726 --> 00:46:15.255
So don't forget to save. So Control + S to save. And here we go.

626
00:46:15.315 --> 00:46:18.915
Next, we're about 70% there. We're going to create some legs.

627
00:46:19.605 --> 00:46:24.585
I'm going to go back to vertex select mode, select a vertex here, generally where

628
00:46:24.616 --> 00:46:29.145
the legs are going to be. Hit X to delete and hit faces. Oops, sorry.

629
00:46:30.705 --> 00:46:33.735
No, I need to... Sorry, let's do three.

630
00:46:34.185 --> 00:46:36.795
For face select, select these four squares.

631
00:46:37.455 --> 00:46:41.145
I'm going to hit X to delete and faces, and I get a hole where the legs are going

632
00:46:41.205 --> 00:46:41.565
to be.

633
00:46:43.605 --> 00:46:47.565
Hit two to do edge select. And here's a new feature.

634
00:46:48.045 --> 00:46:53.476
I can go, and I can select one edge at a time, holding the S, the Shift key to

635
00:46:53.565 --> 00:46:57.525
select all of the edges that generates the loop around this leg.

636
00:46:58.005 --> 00:47:00.435
Or the shortcut key is to hit the Alt key.

637
00:47:00.915 --> 00:47:03.765
I believe on a Mac, that's the Option key, I think.

638
00:47:03.915 --> 00:47:05.445
You guys don't have Alt, you have Option.

639
00:47:06.015 --> 00:47:09.675
If I hold down the Alt key and then select one of the edges, it automatically

640
00:47:09.735 --> 00:47:13.815
selects the entire loop for you. So this is a really, really good time-saver for

641
00:47:13.875 --> 00:47:17.565
you. And the guy's legs are a little bit...

642
00:47:20.325 --> 00:47:21.976
It's round.

643
00:47:24.825 --> 00:47:26.145
Should I do this or should I not?

644
00:47:27.525 --> 00:47:29.145
Yeah. This is a little bit of

645
00:47:30.795 --> 00:47:32.596
advanced technique, but I will show you anyway.

646
00:47:32.685 --> 00:47:38.596
Up at the top here, you have a selection of menus, Mesh, Transform, To Sphere.

647
00:47:39.645 --> 00:47:42.255
And ooh, boy, why am I getting everything?

648
00:47:42.315 --> 00:47:45.195
Okay, proportional editing is still on, so it's affecting everything.

649
00:47:45.735 --> 00:47:50.295
What I want to do is the legs of the Among Us character is round.

650
00:47:50.655 --> 00:47:54.915
And right now, if I extrude this, it's going to create a very square leg, but I

651
00:47:54.945 --> 00:47:58.485
want a round leg. So Mesh, Transform, To Sphere.

652
00:47:58.785 --> 00:48:03.645
And when I move my mouse left and right, it makes that hole a little bit more

653
00:48:03.705 --> 00:48:07.965
round. Okay? So now you can see it's gone from a rectangular shape to a round

654
00:48:07.995 --> 00:48:08.295
shape.

655
00:48:09.855 --> 00:48:13.545
